[{"id":74725,"sender_id":707,"sender_full_name":"Pieter","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/54149905c35938f15f5a72fd6988498b?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Projections/near/74725\">#1</a> I’ve noticed that the page on <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/projection\">projection</a> does not mention the more generic definition of projection used on wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Projection_(mathematics)</p>\n<p>Is there a particular reason for this?</p>","timestamp":1549150042,"subject":"Projections","stream_id":17,"display_recipient":"n-Forum","reactions":[]},{"id":74728,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Projections/near/74728\">#2</a> It’s mentioned in the “linear algebra” section, in the case of vector spaces.  I’ve never heard that terminology used outside of linear algebra, and since the nLab is mainly about category theory the categorical meaning is more relevant.  However, we could certainly mention the other meaning.</p>","timestamp":1549156943,"subject":"Projections","stream_id":17,"display_recipient":"n-Forum","reactions":[]},{"id":74737,"sender_id":707,"sender_full_name":"Pieter","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/54149905c35938f15f5a72fd6988498b?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Projections/near/74737\">#3</a> Hm… I don’t recognize what I mean in the linear algebra section.</p>\n<p>On wikipedia it says that a map <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f : X \\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> is a projection if for any <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span></span></span></span> and map <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g : Z \\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> there exists a map <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h : Z \\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> such that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f h = g</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>That sounds like a categorical definition to me, and seems to have some meaning on its own. Does it go by different names as well, perhaps?</p>","timestamp":1549187383,"subject":"Projections","stream_id":17,"display_recipient":"n-Forum","reactions":[]},{"id":74744,"sender_id":13,"sender_full_name":"Tim_Porter","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f5067a77bfb4640f798f193b25d43eba?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Projections/near/74744\">#4</a> That looks like a split epimorphism by taking <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=\\mathrm{id}_Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">id</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>.</p>","timestamp":1549190463,"subject":"Projections","stream_id":17,"display_recipient":"n-Forum","reactions":[]},{"id":74745,"sender_id":13,"sender_full_name":"Tim_Porter","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f5067a77bfb4640f798f193b25d43eba?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Projections/near/74745\">#5</a> (Duplicate)</p>","timestamp":1549190483,"subject":"Projections","stream_id":17,"display_recipient":"n-Forum","reactions":[]},{"id":74757,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Projections/near/74757\">#6</a> I was looking at the first line of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Projection_(mathematics)\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Projection_(mathematics)</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In mathematics, a projection is a mapping of a set (or other mathematical structure) into a subset (or sub-structure), which is equal to its square for mapping composition (or, in other words, which is idempotent)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I don’t see the definition you mention in <a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Projections/near/74737\">#3</a>.</p>","timestamp":1549208426,"subject":"Projections","stream_id":17,"display_recipient":"n-Forum","reactions":[]},{"id":74762,"sender_id":707,"sender_full_name":"Pieter","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/54149905c35938f15f5a72fd6988498b?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Projections/near/74762\">#7</a> I was looking at the commutativity diagram a bit lower… but I now realize that the definition next to it is a bit more extensive than just this universal property.</p>\n<p>There seems to be a relation with the notion of retract.</p>","timestamp":1549209932,"subject":"Projections","stream_id":17,"display_recipient":"n-Forum","reactions":[]},{"id":74765,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Projections/near/74765\">#8</a> Right, a splitting of an idempotent is an split epimorphism, i.e. a retraction, and any split epimorphism is a splitting of the idempotent induced on its domain.</p>","timestamp":1549214111,"subject":"Projections","stream_id":17,"display_recipient":"n-Forum","reactions":[]}]